WEBVTT

00:05.830 --> 00:07.900
What exactly are numbers?

00:07.930 --> 00:11.980
It seems like such a silly question, but there's actually more to it than you might think.

00:12.070 --> 00:15.550
After all, we're already pretty comfortable thinking about simple numbers.

00:15.550 --> 00:18.280
Like one, two, three, four, five.

00:18.400 --> 00:24.520
But where in our understanding do we put numbers like the radical square root three, the negative number

00:24.520 --> 00:31.930
negative seven, the repeating decimal 4.31 or the improper fraction 17 over 12.

00:32.580 --> 00:36.570
This section will give you a framework for thinking about all different kinds of numbers, including

00:36.570 --> 00:38.100
all of these that we've just listed.

00:38.280 --> 00:44.640
Then we'll really focus in on negative numbers specifically and their relationship to positive numbers.

00:45.090 --> 00:49.950
We'll get started by looking at number sets, which you can think of as the classifications we'll use

00:49.950 --> 00:52.410
for grouping numbers throughout this course.
